{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "scriptId": "pl-01-cost-selling-profit-loss-basics",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Cost Price, Selling Price, Profit and Loss Basics",
    "description": "Understand C.P., S.P., profit and loss, and calculate gain or loss percentages from the correct base.",
    "teachingApproach": "Open with money in versus money out, then build profit/loss amount before percentages. Use a 100-based mental model, direct C.P./S.P. calculations, and ratio forms. Every percentage question names the correct base so the learner stops dividing by S.P. by habit.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 28,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 12, Profit and Loss. Approved scope: Build the meaning of cost price and selling price before formulas. Teach profit = S.P. − C.P. and loss = C.P. − S.P., with gain/loss percentage always reckoned on cost price. Move between C.P., S.P., absolute gain/loss and percentage, including decimal percentages and simple ratio views such as C.P.:S.P. Prepare learners for direct exam questions asking profit percent, loss percent, or whether a transaction is a gain or loss. Use mental 100-based models before general formulas and check by comparing S.P. back with C.P."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's learn cost price, selling price, profit and loss basics.\n\nA shop pays ₹400 for a bag and sells it for ₹460. It earned ₹60 — but what percent profit is that? The answer depends on one important base: the cost price.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A shop buys a bag for ₹400 and sells it for ₹460. The ₹400 is the cost price — what the shop paid. The ₹460 is the selling price — what came back from the customer.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Because ₹460 is above ₹400, there is a profit.\n\nProfit = S.P. − C.P.\n= 460 − 400\n= ₹60\n\nProfit measures the extra money above the cost.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
      "id": "q_1",
      "type": "CHOICE",
      "content": {
        "text": "An article costs ₹500 and sells for ₹575. What is the profit?",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "input": {
        "type": "CHOICE",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "a",
            "label": "575"
          },
          {
            "id": "b",
            "label": "500"
          },
          {
            "id": "c",
            "label": "25"
          },
          {
            "id": "d",
            "label": "75"
          }
        ]
      },
      "questionReference": {
        "mode": "INLINE",
        "inlineData": {
          "questionType": "PRACTICE",
          "prompt": "An article costs ₹500 and sells for ₹575. What is the profit?",
          "options": [
            {
